 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fasting-history-meaning"><h3>What does a fasting tracker actually measure?</h3>
  <p>The <strong>completed count</strong> is made from dates you choose to record, plus any undated completions brought over from the older Sunnah Tools tracker. <strong>Remaining Qada days</strong> is your chosen target minus recorded Qada completions. A voluntary completion never subtracts from Qada. These figures represent <em>your own record</em>, not a verified religious ruling, a mosque-issued statement or an audit of fasting validity.</p>
  <p>Recording a fast involves personal intention and actual observance from true dawn until sunset. A browser can remember a date; it cannot verify intention, health circumstances, exemptions, Fajr timing or what happened during that day. Use the separate <a href="/tools/fasting-times/">Suhoor &amp; Iftar Times</a> calculator for estimated local dawn and sunset.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fasting-history-qada"><h3>What is Qada fasting, and when are make-up fasts needed?</h3>
  <p><a href="https://quran.com/2/184" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:184</a> and <a href="https://quran.com/2/185" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2:185</a> distinguish fasting during Ramadan, making up an equivalent number on other days in specified situations, and feeding-related language. Illness and travel appear directly in the verses. Other circumstances—including menstruation, pregnancy, breastfeeding, permanent inability and an uncertain fasting history—require case-specific religious guidance rather than an automatic rule inferred by a counter.</p>
  <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1950" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1950</a> reports Aisha making up some missed Ramadan fasts during Sha'ban. It demonstrates that make-up fasting is a real, separately discussed practice. It does <strong>not</strong> establish that everyone may delay indefinitely or decide alone whether delayed days also involve feeding; schools and circumstances differ. If your target is uncertain, ask a qualified scholar to help establish a responsible number before entering it.</p>
  <p>Some dates have separate fasting rules. For example, <a href="/tools/white-days-calendar/">White Days</a> are a voluntary pattern and should not be automatically credited to Qada. The tracker keeps the two categories separate without purporting to settle any intention or overlap question.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fasting-history-steps"><h3>How to track missed and completed fasts step by step</h3>
  <ol class="three-tool-steps"><li><strong>Confirm your target.</strong> Enter the count you have established using your own records and qualified guidance, not a generated religious estimate.</li><li><strong>Complete the fast.</strong> The tracker does not record planned dates as completed in advance.</li><li><strong>Select Qada or voluntary.</strong> The distinction prevents a voluntary fast from silently reducing a make-up target.</li><li><strong>Choose the Gregorian date.</strong> The app rejects future dates and duplicated completions for the same day.</li><li><strong>Review your history.</strong> Remove accidental entries and adjust the target without dropping below existing completed Qada days.</li><li><strong>Back up privately.</strong> Save a JSON file for restoration or a CSV/printed copy for your records.</li></ol>
  <p>If you used the old counter, the existing target and completed amount are kept as <strong>undated legacy progress</strong>. They are not assigned imaginary completion dates. You can correct that old undated number one completion at a time. Browser storage can be cleared by the user or browser; backups are opt-in and remain under your control.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fasting-history-example"><h3>Worked example: tracking twelve make-up fasts</h3>
  <div class="three-tool-example"><p>Imagine that, after obtaining suitable guidance, someone enters a <strong>Qada target of 12 days</strong>. The older counter carried over <strong>four undated completed fasts</strong>. They record three further completed Qada dates: the total recorded Qada is now <strong>7</strong> and the tracker shows <strong>5 remaining</strong>.</p><p>If they also enter <strong>two voluntary fasts</strong>, the voluntary counter becomes two while the Qada remaining figure stays <strong>5</strong>. This is an arithmetic example, <strong>not</strong> a ruling that twelve days are owed or that any particular voluntary fast fulfils Qada.</p></div>
  <p>A useful record shows what you actually know, including uncertainty. If historic dates were never recorded, leaving them undated is more accurate than retroactively inventing precise dates.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fasting-history-privacy"><h3>Is your fasting tracker private? Backups, CSV and device changes</h3>
  <p>This tool saves your chosen target and completion entries in the web browser's <strong>local storage</strong>. It does not register an account, send your completion dates to a Sunnah Tools server or compare your worship with anyone else's. A browser profile, shared device or downloaded backup can still be accessed by someone with access to that device; use normal device privacy precautions.</p>
  <ul><li><strong>JSON backup:</strong> records your target, dated completions and any undated legacy total. Imported files must pass strict validation; restoring replaces your current local plan only with your confirmation.</li><li><strong>CSV:</strong> provides a human-readable list of completed dates and category labels, with a separate undated row for older counters.</li><li><strong>Print / Save PDF:</strong> uses your browser's print flow; no PDF is produced or stored on our servers.</li><li><strong>Clear:</strong> requires confirmation because it removes the saved local plan. Export first if you need a copy.</li></ul>
  <p>Do not mistake privacy for permanence. Device loss, private browsing, storage restrictions or clearing cookies/site data can erase local records. Make periodic backups if your own record matters to you.</p>
 </section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="fasting-history-distinctions"><h3>Qada versus Fidya versus Kaffarah: which tool should you use?</h3>
  <div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>Qada</h4><p>A make-up fast in relevant cases. This tracker records the count <em>you</em> establish; it does not determine the duty.</p></div><div><h4>Fidya</h4><p>Feeding-related compensation in specific situations; not every missed fast qualifies. See the <a href="/tools/fidya-calculator/">Fidya Calculator</a>.</p></div><div><h4>Kaffarah</h4><p>Distinct expiations for specified cases such as a deliberate broken oath and certain Ramadan violations. See the <a href="/tools/kaffarah-calculator/">Kaffarah Calculator</a>.</p></div></div>
  <p>No simple checklist can decide eligibility, whether remedies overlap, or which school applies. If illness, pregnancy, breastfeeding or another personal circumstance is relevant, obtain individual religious guidance and appropriate medical advice when health decisions are involved.</p>
 </section>
